---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import { url } from '../../lib/url'

const repo = 'https://github.com/kyletaylored/datadog-rum-community-plugins'
---

<Base title="Contribute" description="Write a plugin for a platform you know, in about a week." docs>
  <h1>Contribute a plugin</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Subscribe to the platform's events, map them to the shared schema, done. Dedupe, the consent
    gate, the PII filter and size caps are all in the core — you write none of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Start</h2>
  <pre><code>{`git clone ${repo}
cd datadog-rum-community-plugins && npm install
npm run new-plugin -- bigcommerce --title "BigCommerce" --family commerce --github your-handle`}</code></pre>
  <p class="small muted">
    The scaffold's tests pass against an empty mapping on purpose, so you start green and watch each
    assertion tighten as you fill it in.
  </p>

  <h2>What you write</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Record real fixtures.</strong> Actual payloads from the platform, trimmed to the fields
      you read, with a note saying where they came from. Include one carrying something that must be
      stripped — a card field, an email — so the privacy rules get exercised, not assumed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Map them</strong> in <code>src/index.ts</code>. Send numbers as numbers. Pass email and
      name straight through and let the core decide; gating them yourself is how one of the two ends
      up wrong.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Declare the footprint</strong> in both <code>FOOTPRINT</code> and
      <code>plugin.json</code>. The suite fails on anything undeclared.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Point the test's <code>emit</code> at your platform</strong> so fixtures reach the
      plugin through its own subscription. Calling the mapper directly skips the wiring most likely
      to be wrong.
    </li>
    <li><code>npm run verify</code></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What will fail your PR</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An action name not in the schema, or a commerce prefix on another family.</li>
    <li>An attribute the schema doesn't define, or the wrong type — a numeric string instead of a number.</li>
    <li>Any blocked key reaching <code>addAction</code> or <code>setUser</code>.</li>
    <li><code>items[]</code> without <code>includeItems</code>, over 20 entries, or with non-GA4 fields.</li>
    <li>More than one <code>commerce.purchase</code> per <code>transaction_id</code>, or one without.</li>
    <li>Anything sent before consent, when a consent source is declared.</li>
    <li>A CDN bundle over 5 KB gzip, or a footprint that doesn't match the run.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Hard rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Never call <code>datadogRum.init</code>.</strong> The customer's init owns the application ID, sampling and privacy. <code>@datadog/browser-rum</code> is a peer dependency, never bundled.</li>
    <li><strong>Public API only.</strong> The core is the one place that touches the SDK's internal plugin type, so an upstream change is one edit, not one per plugin.</li>
    <li><strong>Compose, don't replace.</strong> Where Datadog ships an official plugin, add to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Platform code stays in the plugin.</strong> Writing dedupe or a consent gate means it belongs in the core — open an issue.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tiers</h2>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Bar</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="badge">Community</span></td><td>Valid manifest, passing CI</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="badge verified">Verified</span></td><td>Passes conformance, has a demo, reviewed for the <a href={url('/docs/privacy/')}>privacy rules</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="badge seed">Maintainer</span></td><td>Maintained by the project maintainer</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Each plugin gets a <code>CODEOWNERS</code> entry. No maintainer activity for six months marks it
    deprecated in the gallery rather than leaving it to rot quietly.
  </p>

  <h2>Schema changes</h2>
  <p>
    These affect every plugin and every dashboard built on one, so they need an RFC issue, a semver
    bump, and both halves of the schema updated together.
  </p>

  <h2>Releasing</h2>
  <p>
    Add a changeset with your PR (<code>npm run changeset</code>). Merging to <code>main</code> opens
    a release PR that bumps only what changed; merging that publishes with provenance and computes
    each CDN build's SRI hash.
  </p>

  <div class="next-cards">
    <a class="card" href={url('/docs/schema/')}>
      <h3>Event schema</h3>
      <p>The vocabulary to map onto.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href={url('/docs/privacy/')}>
      <h3>Privacy rules</h3>
      <p>What will fail your PR.</p>
    </a>
  </div>
</Base>
